## Why This Is Asked
New angle on cross-account data access focusing on beginner-friendly, concrete controls, rotation, and rapid containment.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account roles with restricted trusts
- Least-privilege S3 and KMS usage
- CMK rotation and key policy scope
- Breach containment automation (Lambda + EventBridge)

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy for the cross-account role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:role/AnalyticsRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "customer-123"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy allowing only the cross-account role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:role/AnalyticsRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::customer-bucket-123", "arn:aws:s3:::customer-bucket-123/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test no unintended access (rotation, revocation, logs)?
- What monitoring alerts would you wire to detect anomalous cross-account access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of DynamoDB fine-grained access control, tenant isolation, and rapid breach containment in a realistic multi-tenant SaaS scenario.

## Key Concepts
- DynamoDB LeadingKeys for per-tenant access control
- IAM policies with condition keys to enforce least privilege
- SSE-KMS for data-at-rest protection
- CloudTrail data events for DynamoDB auditing
- Rapid containment via revocation of tokens and tenant quarantine

## Code Example
```json
// IAM policy snippet (conceptual)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:GetItem", "dynamodb.PutItem"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:region:account-id:table/TenantTable",
      "Condition": {
        "ForAllValues:StringEquals": {
          "dynamodb:LeadingKeys": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant_id}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the 60-second containment workflow end-to-end?
- What are the trade-offs of a single-table design vs separate tables per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of IAM least-privilege, S3 encryption, presigned URLs, and rapid breach containment.

## Key Concepts
- Least-privilege IAM policies and role separation
- S3 server-side encryption with a KMS key
- Presigned URLs via STS for temporary read access
- Public access blocking at the bucket level
- Audit logging with CloudTrail; breach containment plan

## Code Example
```json
// Bucket policy (deny public access and allow app-specific principals)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket",
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Lambda execution role trust (example)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test key rotation and ensure no downtime?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect misconfigurations or drift?
